  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first: plain steps overshoot above learning rate 0.33 and grow past 0.67.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per direction has spread 2·√((64−b)/(63b)) for batch b: 2 at b=1, exactly 0 at b=64. On average it points downhill; single steps can raise the loss.",
    "Both arrows use one scale: drawn length = 3 × the step length. Orange is the next mini-batch step (the way the ball moves); grey is −learning rate × true slope.",
    "Status: diverged when the loss passes 6 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl; the run then stops. Finished after 40 steps. The loss readout always shows the true value.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed in order, run by run; Reset clears both paths and replays the same sequence. Running takes 5 steps per second at speed 1."
  ]
}